{"chunk_id": "179789c5c4bd", "text": "This section explains the myth behind Vishnu\u2019s horse\u2011headed form (Hayasiras/Hayagriva) and the recovery of the Vedas. King Janamejaya asks why the Supreme Lord once appeared with an equine head. Vaisampayana responds by describing cosmic dissolution and creation: the elements merge successively until only the Supreme remains, from whom Brahma emerges upon the lotus. Two demons, Madhu and Kaitabha, born from drops within the lotus and embodying the qualities of Tamas and Rajas, steal the Vedas from Brahma and hide them in the cosmic waters. In despair, Brahma praises Narayana, who awakens from yogic sleep, assumes a radiant horse\u2011headed form embodying the cosmos, retrieves the Vedas, and later slays the demons. The passage emphasizes Narayana as the ultimate source, sustainer, and inner soul of all phenomena, presenting a theological vision in which all elements, philosophies, and cosmic processes culminate in him.\nJanamejaya asks about Vishnu\u2019s horse\u2011headed form; the tale recounts how Narayana as Hayagriva recovers the stolen Vedas from the demons Madhu and Kaitabha.\nThemes: divine cosmology, Vedas as sacred knowledge, Vishnu avatars, creation and dissolution, battle with Madhu and Kaitabha", "vector": [-0.006647036410868168, -5.990346835460514e-05, -0.0033595010172575712, -0.0100490627810359, -0.02361791394650936, -0.008786387741565704, -0.014445527456700802, -0.028995731845498085, -0.009872418828308582, -0.0036931612994521856, 0.01701013185083866, 0.004972192458808422, -0.003074908396229148, 0.0036669918335974216, -0.0031517811585217714, 0.0015309118898585439, 0.035224057734012604, -0.017441928386688232, 0.029309766367077827, -0.0007396953878924251, 0.004059533588588238, -0.009931299835443497, -0.009420996531844139, 0.01673535257577896, -0.014681052416563034, 0.019077517092227936, 0.031795863062143326, -0.022610390558838844, 0.00836767628788948, -0.02253188192844391, -0.006738629192113876, 0.00699378